WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:13.228 --> 00:00:17.863
Personnel economics is the application
of economic principles,

00:00:17.863 --> 00:00:21.257
statistics, economic theory, mathematics,

00:00:21.257 --> 00:00:26.240
even, to questions that were
traditional human resource questions.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.500
So questions that people would not
normally think of as lending themselves

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.260
easily to economics, motivating workers,

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.190
hierarchical kinds of questions, how much
authority should you give to your workers?

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.997
Those issues that were traditional in
human resources were dealt with in

00:00:43.997 --> 00:00:45.584
quite a different way.

00:00:45.584 --> 00:00:49.098
So if you think about organizational
behavior, which is another field in our

00:00:49.098 --> 00:00:52.396
business school and in most business
schools, they think of it more from

00:00:52.396 --> 00:00:55.800
the psychology point of view or
sometimes sociology point of view.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.780
Whereas I deal with it, and to be honest,
I would say a harder-nosed way,

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.280
more mathematical, more statistical way.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.621
And that's what personnel
economics is about.

00:01:06.621 --> 00:01:10.723
[MUSIC]

00:01:10.723 --> 00:01:15.628
If you look across countries, and
you say, in which countries have

00:01:15.628 --> 00:01:20.980
workers enjoyed the highest wage gains,
say, over the past 15 years?

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.249
And then you say,

00:01:22.249 --> 00:01:27.748
in which countries have workers been
most productive in the past 15 years,

00:01:27.748 --> 00:01:34.100
what you'll find is that the correlation
between those two is very, very strong.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:39.025
What that means is that if you go across
countries and you look at these countries

00:01:39.025 --> 00:01:43.730
that are being effective in raising
worker productivity, are also the ones

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:48.387
where workers are seeing their wages grow,
so that's really important.

00:01:48.387 --> 00:01:54.272
[MUSIC]

00:01:54.272 --> 00:01:57.301
One of the most traditional
ways to motivate workers and

00:01:57.301 --> 00:02:00.460
to increase both their productivity and
their earnings,

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.372
is to pay them something
that's called a peace rate.

00:02:03.372 --> 00:02:06.820
Most of us are familiar with peace rates.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:08.830
There are a couple of obvious examples.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:11.400
Uber is a peace rate,
why is Uber a peace rate?

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:18.350
Well, because you basically get paid for
the number of rides that you give.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.400
Depends on the amount of time
that you spend driving, but

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.870
more than that it depends on the actual
amount of miles that you drive.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.860
And so they're paying you on
the basis of your output,

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.050
that's a very effective
way to motivate people.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.970
And of course, if you look at Uber
drivers, they're pretty darn motivated.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.406
They work pretty hard,
they work long hours.

00:02:36.406 --> 00:02:41.188
They are very good at figuring out
the system, how best to get rides,

00:02:41.188 --> 00:02:44.167
where to locate at certain points in time.

00:02:44.167 --> 00:02:46.908
What does that for them,
the fact that they are directly paid on

00:02:46.908 --> 00:02:49.455
the basis of their output,
so that's a great motivator.

00:02:49.455 --> 00:02:55.270
[MUSIC]

00:02:55.270 --> 00:03:01.020
How do you motivate people in situations
where output is difficult to measure?

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:04.240
Most of those kinds of
jobs are managerial jobs,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.300
and managerial jobs are important for
the economy,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.190
because they are the basis
of much of our productivity.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.173
It's not just the workers, it's
the people who are managing the workers,

00:03:14.173 --> 00:03:16.230
supervising them,
teaching them, guiding them.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.270
How do you motivate the teachers and
the motivators?

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.520
Essentially what you have to do is,

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:26.722
you have to pay on the basis
of relative performance.

00:03:26.722 --> 00:03:30.697
So something that i came up with
a long time ago, again, in my youth,

00:03:30.697 --> 00:03:32.696
it's called tournament theory.

00:03:32.696 --> 00:03:38.369
[MUSIC]

00:03:38.369 --> 00:03:42.026
What tournament theory is about is, it's
kind of obvious when you think about it,

00:03:42.026 --> 00:03:46.105
it's not very complicated, you kind of
wonder why no one thought about it before.

00:03:46.105 --> 00:03:50.885
But it's basically the idea that
firms structure their compensation

00:03:50.885 --> 00:03:54.905
in the same way that people run
tournaments, sports tournaments.

00:03:54.905 --> 00:03:59.170
So for example, if you think about
Wimbledon, tennis match, and

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.440
you say, how does Wimbledon work?

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.940
Well, Wimbledon works by paying a prize
to the winner, and second place prize

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:11.210
to the loser of the finals, because that's
the second place person in the tournament.

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:13.470
The difference between
the winner's prize and

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.430
the losers prize serves to
motivate those players.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.770
Now they would be motivated even if
they didn't have that prize, but

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:22.880
depending on the size of that prize,
they'll be motivated even more.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:28.002
So for example, if there were a million
pounds at stake winning that tournament,

00:04:28.002 --> 00:04:30.685
people would be highly motivated to play.

00:04:30.685 --> 00:04:33.122
And that's what happens in business.

00:04:33.122 --> 00:04:36.674
So what tournament theory talks about is,
first of all,

00:04:36.674 --> 00:04:41.781
how to set up those implicit contests,
competition between these workers, do it

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:47.200
in a way that doesn't undermine the firm,
because sometimes competition can be bad.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.967
They'll work to sabotage
one another essentially,

00:04:49.967 --> 00:04:52.679
because they're trying to
get the next promotion.

00:04:52.679 --> 00:04:56.903
And then finally to think about how big
a raise should you give those individuals

00:04:56.903 --> 00:05:01.064
when they get promoted from one level to
another to both motivate them below and

00:05:01.064 --> 00:05:02.913
then give them incentives above?

00:05:02.913 --> 00:05:08.533
[MUSIC]

00:05:08.533 --> 00:05:13.357
Another way that workers are sometimes
compensated is on the basis of the team,

00:05:13.357 --> 00:05:14.880
why would you do that?

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.580
Well, sometimes people need to cooperate,
and in order to get them to cooperate,

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.500
you have to pay them on
the basis of their joint output,

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.750
not on the basis of
their individual output.

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:26.660
Let me give you an example.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.643
There's a good example by one of my
colleagues here, Catherine Shah,

00:05:31.643 --> 00:05:36.541
who with a couple of co-authors did
a very nice study of a firm in Brazil

00:05:36.541 --> 00:05:38.632
that was actually a law firm.

00:05:38.632 --> 00:05:43.208
And what they did was, they had initially
paid their lawyers on the basis of their

00:05:43.208 --> 00:05:48.004
billings, straight on the on the number
of billings, that's a measure of output.

00:05:48.004 --> 00:05:52.205
Sounds great, because you can easily
observe it, you basically know who's doing

00:05:52.205 --> 00:05:56.540
what, you can motivate them very
effectively, but here's the problem.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:01.510
Because they were being paid on the basis
of their own billings, the senior lawyers,

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:04.840
who had the ability to
allocate cases to one another,

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.870
took all the great cases for themselves,

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:10.620
even when they weren't necessarily
the best partner to handle the job.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:14.710
And so what the firm did is, they said,
our junior people, some of whom

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:19.210
are highly qualified to be working on
these cases, are not getting the work,

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:21.720
because the senior guys
are taking it all for themselves.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:22.718
What do we do?

00:06:22.718 --> 00:06:26.275
We've gotta change the compensation
structure so that you're paid not only on

00:06:26.275 --> 00:06:31.165
your own billings, but you're essentially
paid on the profitability of the firm.

00:06:31.165 --> 00:06:34.645
Now, because you're paid on
the profitability of the whole firm or

00:06:34.645 --> 00:06:38.760
of a sector of the firm, you care
not only about how well you do, but

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.270
you also care about how
well your coworkers do.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:47.420
As a result, there was an enormous
transformation in the way that work was

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:51.660
allocated, and an enormous transformation
as well in the productivity of the firm.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.623
So profits went up,
billings actually went up, and

00:06:55.623 --> 00:07:01.397
the work was better handled and people
were much happier as a result of that.

00:07:01.397 --> 00:07:04.823
[MUSIC]

00:07:04.823 --> 00:07:07.093
Team compensation can be very effective.

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:11.256
One problem with team compensation
is it doesn't always work, why not?

00:07:11.256 --> 00:07:14.751
There's something in economics
called the free rider problem,

00:07:14.751 --> 00:07:17.747
I'm sure many of your listeners
have heard about that.

00:07:17.747 --> 00:07:23.975
But the free rider problem comes about
when you have an incentive to shirk and

00:07:23.975 --> 00:07:27.487
basically put the burden on someone else.

00:07:27.487 --> 00:07:30.722
Some of your viewers may remember
when they were in school,

00:07:30.722 --> 00:07:35.518
perhaps even at Stanford Business School,
although we don't have any shirkers here.

00:07:35.518 --> 00:07:38.350
But if you're involved in a team project,

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:41.576
suppose five of you have
a homework assignment,

00:07:41.576 --> 00:07:46.990
you have to get together and write up
the assignment for the whole team.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:50.150
Sometimes one person will work
harder than another person, and

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:54.750
sometimes even a teammate might be
critical of the other teammate,

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.640
because of the behavior
of the other teammate,

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:02.460
not putting in the appropriate amount of
effort, the appropriate amount of time.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.931
So the free rider effect comes about in
teams, and as the team size gets larger,

00:08:06.931 --> 00:08:09.690
the free rider effect
becomes more pronounced.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:15.013
Because your ability to affect the total
output of the team gets smaller and

00:08:15.013 --> 00:08:16.370
smaller.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:20.390
So team output,
team compensation can be affected

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:25.390
in a strong way as long as the teams
are structured appropriately.

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:29.010
First of all, you have to make
sure that the teams Involve

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:32.560
people who are highly
complimentary to one another.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:35.300
So you want to make sure that
I'm working with someone whose

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:39.620
output will benefit greatly
from my input and vice versa.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.499
You also want to make sure that
the team is small enough so

00:08:42.499 --> 00:08:45.564
that we can observe each other,
we can monitor each other,

00:08:45.564 --> 00:08:49.388
we can motivate each other, and
that's an important component as well.

00:08:49.388 --> 00:08:53.593
So firms can use team compensation, but

00:08:53.593 --> 00:08:59.660
they have to be pretty careful
about the way they set it up.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:00.220
[MUSIC]

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:04.734
People often work for a long time in
a company, and the question is, what do

00:09:04.734 --> 00:09:09.550
you do to motivate managers when they're
essentially done with a tournament?

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:11.030
What do I mean by done
with the tournament?

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:16.580
Look at me, I've been the equivalent of a
middle level manager for most of my life.

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:20.104
I'm a senior professor at Stanford,
but there's nowhere for me to go.

00:09:20.104 --> 00:09:23.607
I have no aspirations to be a dean or
anything like that.

00:09:23.607 --> 00:09:25.565
So how do you keep me motivated?

00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:30.951
Well, again, there are ways that you can
do this that have to do with compensation

00:09:30.951 --> 00:09:37.240
in addition to non-monetary forms, but let
me give you the one that's compensation.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:41.034
So when I was a young guy, and I was
working really hard trying to get tenure,

00:09:41.034 --> 00:09:43.542
at this point it was at
the University of Chicago.

00:09:43.542 --> 00:09:47.897
And I was looking at my senior colleagues,
and while they were very smart and

00:09:47.897 --> 00:09:52.186
very productive people, I still thought
that I was working a lot harder and

00:09:52.186 --> 00:09:53.565
even more productive.

00:09:53.565 --> 00:09:56.965
I wrote a lot of papers in those days,
I still try to write a lot of papers.

00:09:56.965 --> 00:10:01.375
But when I was a young person,
I was probably better at it than I am now.

00:10:01.375 --> 00:10:04.991
And yet I was paid a lot less than
my senior colleagues, and I thought,

00:10:04.991 --> 00:10:07.032
why is that, isn't this kind of crazy?

00:10:07.032 --> 00:10:10.471
I'm the guy doing all the work, and
they're the guys making all the money.

00:10:10.471 --> 00:10:14.155
And so I thought about it and
I realized, well, actually,

00:10:14.155 --> 00:10:17.841
that motivates me,
because what happens is, as a result of

00:10:17.841 --> 00:10:22.807
seeing my senior colleagues make a lot of
money, I want to be a senior colleague.

00:10:22.807 --> 00:10:25.222
And that was true throughout
most of my career.

00:10:25.222 --> 00:10:27.168
So when I was in my 30s and 40s,

00:10:27.168 --> 00:10:31.133
at that point I was already a tenured
professor with an endowed chair,

00:10:31.133 --> 00:10:34.758
there was pretty much nowhere for
me to go in the tournament, but

00:10:34.758 --> 00:10:38.411
I still cared a lot about being
successful in the organization.

00:10:38.411 --> 00:10:43.221
Not only because I wanted Stanford to
succeed and I wanted myself to succeed,

00:10:43.221 --> 00:10:46.773
but also because I wanted to
be well treated in my old age,

00:10:46.773 --> 00:10:48.415
which I have now reached.

00:10:48.415 --> 00:10:53.608
And I am well treated, and I think part
of that is because I try to continue

00:10:53.608 --> 00:10:59.330
to be productive, but part of that is also
pay off for what I did as a younger man.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:04.314
And that motivates the younger generation,
it motivates the 40 and 50 year olds, many

00:11:04.314 --> 00:11:08.922
of whom are still very productive, and we
want to keep those people very productive.

00:11:08.922 --> 00:11:13.701
By their ability to see how Stanford
treats me, they say, all right,

00:11:13.701 --> 00:11:18.318
if I perform well, I understand
that someday I'll be like Lazear,

00:11:18.318 --> 00:11:22.287
and maybe I'll be a little bit overpaid,
but it's okay,

00:11:22.287 --> 00:11:25.781
it's compensation for
what I'm doing right now.

00:11:25.781 --> 00:11:32.070
[MUSIC]

